=== L1 Plot and Structure: Four Seasons, Four Children ===
id: 9df63447-98d9-4ec1-b5df-0c0e16362406
slug: plot-and-structure-four-seasons-four-children
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:06.832789+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How Bovell shapes the play around the seasons and gives each child their own central revelation.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">A Year in the Price Garden</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Andrew Bovell's <em>Things I Know to Be True</em> is structured around the four seasons of a single year. Each season belongs primarily to one of the four adult Price children &mdash; Pip in spring, Mark in summer, Ben in autumn, Rosie in winter &mdash; and each season delivers a revelation that unsettles the suburban family. This seasonal frame is not decorative. It is the play's <dfn class="term" data-def="The way a playwright organises the action of a play, including act/scene divisions, framing devices and the sequencing of events.">dramatic structure</dfn>: four interlocking acts of unequal emotional weight, building cumulatively towards a shock at the play's close that retrospectively re-orders what the audience believed they understood.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">The structure is broadly chronological, moving through a calendar year. Yet within each season, time is not always linear. Bovell inserts <dfn class="term" data-def="A speech in which a character speaks directly to the audience, stepping outside the action of the scene.">direct address</dfn> monologue passages, particularly through the voice of Rosie, who acts as a narrator-figure. These moments allow characters to speak from a future perspective, as if looking back on what the season revealed, creating a layer of dramatic irony that gives the audience information slightly ahead of the other characters.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name the four seasons in order and which child is foregrounded in each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four seasons map onto four children: spring (Pip), summer (Mark), autumn (Ben), winter (Rosie). Each season brings a revelation that tests the Price family's sense of who they are and what holds them together.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">The Kitchen and the Garden as Twin Centres</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">The action takes place primarily in two spaces: the Price family kitchen and the suburban rose garden Bob tends throughout the year. The garden is both a literal and symbolic space. As a <dfn class="term" data-def="An object, image or element of staging that carries significance beyond its literal function, representing an idea, relationship or emotional state.">symbol</dfn>, it holds Bob's emotional life: his sense of order, control and beauty in a world where his children's revelations keep threatening to undermine the certainties he has built. On stage, the garden can be established simply &mdash; a few rose bushes, a soil patch, a garden chair &mdash; and the <dfn class="term" data-def="The arrangement and movement of performers on stage throughout a performance.">blocking</dfn> often places Bob alone there before another character arrives to disturb his peace.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">The kitchen, meanwhile, is the domestic centre: Fran's territory, associated with food, practicality and direct speech. In production, the two spaces can coexist on stage simultaneously, so that a director's choice about <dfn class="term" data-def="The position and arrangement of performers relative to each other and to the audience, used to express status, relationship and emotional state.">proxemics</dfn> between characters moving between kitchen and garden becomes itself a staging language. When a child crosses from the kitchen to join Bob in the garden, the move marks a shift in emotional register.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why the Seasonal Structure Matters in the Exam</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">A common student error is to retell the four revelations as plot summary. Examiners reward analysis of <em>why</em> Bovell chose this structure. Consider what the seasonal frame achieves: each revelation lands at a different emotional temperature. Spring carries the energy of new beginnings; Pip's departure is therefore against the grain of hope. Autumn's association with decay and harvest perfectly frames Ben's arrest. Winter's coldness amplifies the shock of the play's ending.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n9">The structural symmetry also allows Bovell to make each child's season resonate against the others. The audience accumulates information across the year, so by winter they carry the emotional weight of every previous season. As a designer, you might reflect this accumulation through a gradually changing lighting palette &mdash; brighter, warmer in spring, progressively more stripped and cool by winter &mdash; creating a visual arc that reinforces the structural one.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Frantic Assembly Movement Within the Seasonal Frame</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">The play was co-developed with Frantic Assembly, the British physical theatre company, and their influence shapes where moments of stylised movement are inserted into the seasonal structure. At emotional peaks &mdash; moments too large for naturalistic dialogue &mdash; the characters enter Frantic Assembly movement sequences: <dfn class="term" data-def="A Frantic Assembly movement technique in which two performers use chairs as props, creating lifts, slides and counter-balance sequences.">chair duets</dfn>, contact lifts and round-by-through-under partner work. These sequences do not replace the dialogue; they abstracted the emotion that dialogue cannot fully express.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">In structural terms, these movement passages function like the heightened soliloquies in classical drama: they signal that the play is stepping outside its naturalistic register to allow the audience to feel rather than analyse. As a performer, you must understand where you are in the seasonal structure when one of these sequences begins &mdash; the physical demands of a spring chair duet carry a different emotional charge from a winter one.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Without looking, explain in two sentences why Bovell inserts Frantic Assembly movement sequences into an otherwise naturalistic family drama.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Frantic Assembly movement sequences interrupt the naturalistic dialogue at moments of heightened emotion. They are structural devices, not decoration: they show what characters feel but cannot say.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">The Shock Ending: Dramatic Irony in Retrospect</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Bovell builds a late-play revelation about Bob &mdash; a revelation that casts new light on everything that has preceded it. This is a structural device Bovell uses across his work: the thing you think you know is retrospectively undone. The audience carries their understanding of Bob through all four seasons and then, in winter, discovers they have been watching with incomplete information.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">For a director, the challenge is to stage the ending so that the <dfn class="term" data-def="A still image created by performers holding positions, used to encapsulate a moment of significance or to frame the end of a scene.">tableau</dfn> at the play's close resonates with every earlier tableau the audience has seen. The ending must feel both surprising and, in retrospect, inevitable &mdash; the hallmark of precisely engineered dramatic structure. In production, designers often use a sudden, stark shift in the <dfn class="term" data-def="The quality, colour and intensity of light on stage at a given moment.">lighting state</dfn> to mark the revelation: a wash that was warm and amber throughout the summer season becomes a cold, bare white or a near-blackout, so that the structural shock is felt as a physical change in the space.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: The Monologues Are Just Narration</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">Students sometimes treat the direct-address passages as straightforward narration &mdash; information delivery. In fact, they are structurally complex. Rosie's monologues are spoken from a temporal perspective that is partly ahead of where the action has reached. This means the audience is in a state of partial dramatic irony: they sense that things will go wrong before they go wrong. As a performer playing Rosie, the challenge is to hold both timeframes in your body: the innocence of the present tense and the knowledge of the future. Physically, this might mean a stillness that contrasts with the movement of the scenes around her, positioning her slightly apart &mdash; at <dfn class="term" data-def="The area of the stage furthest from the audience.">upstage</dfn> centre, separated from the family activity downstage &mdash; to suggest her dual temporal position.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul><li data-narration-id="n21">Bovell structures the play around four seasons, each foregrounding one child's revelation, building cumulative emotional weight towards a late-play shock that re-orders what the audience believed.</li><li data-narration-id="n22">The kitchen and the rose garden are twin centres: the garden is a recurring symbol of Bob's emotional life; proxemics between these spaces carries meaning for a director.</li><li data-narration-id="n23">Frantic Assembly movement sequences are structural devices, not decoration: they mark the moments where naturalistic dialogue cannot hold the emotion, and a designer's lighting choices should reflect the seasonal arc throughout.</li></ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">For the 12-mark question asking you to analyse playwright intentions, the most common error is describing what happens in each season rather than explaining <em>why</em> Bovell chose to structure the play this way. Ask yourself: what does the seasonal frame allow Bovell to do that a conventional act structure would not? How does the accumulation of revelations create a specific effect on the audience by the time the final shock lands? Your answer must refer to what the playwright wants the audience to think or feel at that moment &mdash; not just what the characters do.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">For the 20-mark extended staging response, remember you must address both performer and designer choices. A strong answer for a plot-and-structure question might discuss: the lighting state transitions between seasons (designer), the use of upstage/downstage space for the garden and kitchen (director/set designer), and the vocal register a performer uses in a direct-address monologue compared to a naturalistic dialogue scene (performer). All three must be anchored to specific named moments in the play.</p>

