=== L2 Characters: The Price Family ===
id: 2b2ec1ed-2204-417b-814d-2e48cff237d3
slug: characters-the-price-family
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:12:59.572878+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 24

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Bob and Fran as parents, and Pip, Mark, Ben and Rosie navigating identity and their parents' expectations.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Price Family: Six Characters, Six Truths</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">At the centre of <em>Things I Know to Be True</em> are six characters whose individual arcs intersect and collide across one year. Bovell constructs each character through a combination of <dfn class="term" data-def="The technique of creating a believable character through observation, psychological truth and an understanding of given circumstances.">naturalistic characterisation</dfn> and carefully placed revelation. None of the six is a simple figure. The parents have their own secrets; the children's crises are not resolved neatly. Understanding each character's motivation is essential for answering performer-focused questions and for analysing how Bovell stages the tensions between them.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Bob Price &mdash; The Father</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Bob is a retired man whose identity is bound up in the rose garden. He is quietly principled and emotionally guarded: a man who loves his family but finds direct emotional expression difficult. On stage, Bob's relationship with the garden anchors his <dfn class="term" data-def="The use of space and distance between performers to express relationship and status.">proxemics</dfn>: when he is in the garden alone, he is at his most settled. When a child intrudes into that space, his physical stillness &mdash; a characteristic upright posture, hands engaged with the plants &mdash; can shift to a sudden tension in the shoulders, a hesitation before he turns. As a performer playing Bob, you would use a slower pace and lower vocal register to convey his measured, restrained quality, contrasting sharply with the moments when emotion breaks through his composure.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Bob carries a secret that is not fully revealed until winter. A performer must therefore play Bob with a layer of contained guilt or private knowledge beneath the surface of his public manner, without signalling the secret prematurely. This is classic Stanislavskian work: knowing the character's given circumstances in full, even when the audience does not yet know them.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name two specific vocal or physical choices a performer might use to show Bob's emotional guardedness in the rose-garden scenes.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Bob is emotionally guarded and principled, defined by his attachment to the rose garden. A performer must carry the weight of a concealed revelation while sustaining the surface of a loving but reserved father.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Fran Price &mdash; The Mother</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Fran is a nurse: direct-spoken, pragmatic and tougher than Bob in her immediate emotional responses. She is the domestic authority of the kitchen, and Bovell frequently stages her interactions with the children in that space, where her command is physical and verbal. As a performer, Fran requires a brisk, purposeful quality: a precise, clipped delivery that can suddenly crack when she is confronted with something she is not prepared for. The contrast between her professional competence &mdash; a nurse who handles crisis daily &mdash; and her shock at her children's revelations is a key dramatic irony that a performer can exploit.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">In staging terms, a lighting designer might use a warmer, directed <dfn class="term" data-def="A focused beam of light designed to illuminate a single performer or area of the stage.">lighting special</dfn> on Fran in kitchen scenes to reinforce her as the emotional centre of this domestic space, contrasting with the more diffuse exterior quality of the garden lighting.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">The Four Children</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Each child is given a distinct arc and a contrasting relationship to their parents' expectations. <strong>Pip</strong>, the eldest, is leaving her marriage and her city; her revelation is about her own agency and the cost of self-determination. Her scenes with Fran carry the deepest confrontation between the women: the mother who stayed and the daughter who is leaving. A performer playing Pip must convey both the guilt of abandonment and the urgency of self-liberation &mdash; often simultaneously, in a single scene.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12"><strong>Mark</strong> undergoes gender transition and returns as Mia. Bovell treats this arc with careful dramatic attention. The script charts the journey across the summer season, showing how each member of the family responds differently to a transformation they may not have expected. As a performer, the challenge is to play Mark's revelation with specificity: the particular mixture of vulnerability and assertion that comes from speaking a truth about yourself to people whose acceptance matters most. Physically, the movement sequences around this arc can carry a quality of both constraint and release, as Frantic Assembly's contact work is used to express what the dialogue cannot.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13"><strong>Ben</strong>, the corporate financier, has been embezzling. His autumn revelation is marked by a different register: the shame of exposure in a family that values hard work and honesty. Ben's scenes with Bob carry the burden of a son seeking his father's judgement. A performer might use a tightly controlled stillness that gradually deteriorates &mdash; a tightening of the jaw, a slower and more effortful pace of speech &mdash; as the confession progresses.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14"><strong>Rosie</strong>, the youngest daughter, returns from Berlin heartbroken. She is the play's narrator-figure: the character who speaks directly to the audience and who appears to hold knowledge the other characters do not. Her <dfn class="term" data-def="The relationship between a performer and the audience, created through eye contact, physical orientation and the breaking of the performance frame.">performer&ndash;audience relationship</dfn> is more openly mediated than the others'. In staging, Rosie is often placed at <dfn class="term" data-def="The area of the stage furthest from the audience.">upstage</dfn> centre or slightly apart from the family groupings when she addresses the audience, so that her dual role &mdash; participant in the family drama and witness to it &mdash; is visible in the <dfn class="term" data-def="The arrangement and movement of performers on stage throughout a performance.">blocking</dfn>.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Performer Focus: Playing Mark / Mia with Specificity</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Examiners reward performers who answer character questions with concrete vocal and physical specificity. For the Mark / Mia arc, a strong answer avoids generalising and instead describes distinct physical and vocal choices for distinct moments. For example: in the scene where Mark speaks to the parents, a performer might use a measured vocal pace with deliberate pauses, a sustained level gaze at each parent in turn rather than looking away, and an open but grounded posture &mdash; weight through both feet, hands relaxed &mdash; to communicate both the vulnerability of the moment and the assertion of self-knowledge. This is very different from the physicality of Ben's shame-filled confession, which calls for more restricted, self-protective gestures.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n16">The <dfn class="term" data-def="The physical distance between two performers on stage, which communicates their emotional and social relationship.">proxemic distance</dfn> between Mark and each parent at this moment is itself a staging decision: how close does Bob stand? Does Fran move towards her child or remain behind the kitchen counter? These choices communicate the parents' responses before any dialogue is spoken.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="Cover this and name a specific vocal quality and a specific physical choice you would use to distinguish Fran's manner from Bob's in the opening family scene.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The six Price family members each require distinct vocal and physical characterisation. Bovell builds contrast into the family's dynamics: Bob (restrained, guarded), Fran (direct, purposeful), Pip (urgent, guilty), Mark (vulnerable yet assertive), Ben (controlled, deteriorating), Rosie (narrator-witness).</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: The Parents Are a Unified Unit</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Students sometimes treat Bob and Fran as a single parental bloc &mdash; two people who respond identically to their children's revelations. Bovell does not write them that way. They have different emotional registers, different relationships with each child, and, crucially, different secrets. In staging, a director might use the physical geography of the kitchen and garden to reinforce this: Fran rarely enters the garden (her territory is the kitchen), so when she does cross into Bob's space, the <dfn class="term" data-def="The position and arrangement of performers relative to each other and to the audience, used to express status, relationship and emotional state.">proxemics</dfn> of that crossing signals a shift in the power dynamic between them.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n21">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul><li data-narration-id="n22">Each of the six Price family members requires distinct vocal and physical characterisation: Bob is guarded and principled; Fran is direct and purposeful; each child is defined by the nature of their revelation and their relationship to parental expectation.</li><li data-narration-id="n23">Mark / Mia's arc demands performer specificity at each moment of the journey; examiners reward concrete vocal and physical choices anchored to the specific point in the arc, not generalised sentiment.</li><li data-narration-id="n24">The kitchen / garden geography gives a director a powerful proxemics language: which characters enter which space, and when, communicates shifts in power and emotional alignment before a word is spoken.</li></ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">For the 8-mark performer question, the most common error is describing what the character is feeling without translating it into specific vocal and physical choices at a named moment. &ldquo;I would act nervously&rdquo; earns no marks. Instead: &ldquo;As a performer playing Ben in the confession scene with Bob, I would use a slower pace of speech, dropping my volume to near-whisper, while physically turning slightly away from Bob &mdash; a three-quarter profile that suggests both the wish to speak and the instinct to withdraw.&rdquo; Every performer answer needs a named moment, a specific vocal quality and a specific physical choice.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n20">For the 20-mark extended staging response, the question may focus on a specific character interaction. Use the kitchen / garden geography to anchor your design choices: lighting can distinguish the two spaces, costume can signal character status and register, and the proxemics between characters in a given scene are the director's most powerful vocabulary for communicating family dynamics.</p>

