=== L4 Context: Bovell, Frantic Assembly and 2016 Adelaide ===
id: 8d4cd7e1-e67c-4754-b4c5-3be9671f4644
slug: context-bovell-frantic-assembly-and-2016-adelaide
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:41:03.847931+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 24

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Bovell's career, the Frantic Assembly co-production and the play's contemporary Australian setting.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Andrew Bovell: Playwright of Family Secrets</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Andrew Bovell was born in 1962 in Australia. He is one of the most significant Australian playwrights of his generation, working across theatre and screen. His theatre writing is characterised by an interest in family, secrets, and the long shadow of the past on the present. His play <em>Speaking in Tongues</em> (1996) was adapted into the acclaimed film <em>Lantana</em>. His <em>When the Rain Stops Falling</em> (2008) traces a family across four generations and two continents, exploring the way inherited secrets reverberate through time.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Understanding Bovell's broader work helps explain his approach to <em>Things I Know to Be True</em>. He is not a playwright interested in simple moral resolution. His families are flawed, his parents are fallible, and his plays typically end with a revelation that refuses to tidy itself away. In staging terms, this means a designer must plan for a production that does not deliver comfort: the <dfn class="term" data-def="The quality, colour and intensity of light on stage at a given moment.">lighting state</dfn> at the end of a Bovell play is more likely to strip the stage bare than to bathe it in warmth.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name one other Bovell play and say in one sentence what connects its subject matter to Things I Know to Be True.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Andrew Bovell (born 1962, Australia) is known for plays about family secrets and inherited trauma. <em>When the Rain Stops Falling</em> (2008) and <em>Speaking in Tongues</em> (1996) are his other major theatre works. The AQA set play is published in the Nick Hern Modern Plays edition.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Frantic Assembly: Physical Theatre as Structural Language</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="A style of theatre that places physical movement and the body at the centre of performance, often integrating gesture, dance and contact work with or instead of text.">Physical theatre</dfn> is a performance mode in which the body carries meaning as centrally as spoken language. Frantic Assembly, the British company co-founded by Scott Graham and Steven Hoggett in 1994, is one of the defining physical theatre companies of their generation. Their method of building productions blends text with devised movement, and their signature vocabulary includes <dfn class="term" data-def="A Frantic Assembly movement technique in which two performers use chairs as props, creating lifts, slides and counter-balance sequences.">chair duets</dfn>, partner lifts, round-by-through-under sequences and contact improvisation work.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">What makes <em>Things I Know to Be True</em> distinctive in the AQA set play list is that it was co-developed with Frantic Assembly. Bovell and Scott Graham worked on the play together: the movement sequences are not choreographed additions to a finished text but are built into the play's structure at the writing stage. The stage directions specify where these sequences occur and broadly describe their physical quality. This integration means that Frantic Assembly's aesthetic is not decorative but structural: a production of the play that removed the movement sequences would be a different play.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8">For a student approaching the play as a designer or director, the Frantic Assembly context has direct practical implications. The movement sequences require a stage space that allows full-body contact work: a very cluttered set or a small thrust stage would limit their impact. A production on a wide <dfn class="term" data-def="A stage configuration in which the stage and audience face each other across a wide playing space, with the stage elevated behind a picture-frame opening.">proscenium arch</dfn> stage can use the depth of the stage effectively, placing the movement sequences in a broader upstage space while naturalistic dialogue scenes play in a more intimate downstage area.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Stanislavski and Frantic Assembly: A Hybrid Practice</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9">Bovell's approach to character sits firmly in the <dfn class="term" data-def="A system of actor training developed by Constantin Stanislavski that focuses on psychological truth, given circumstances, emotional memory and the pursuit of a character's objective.">Stanislavskian tradition</dfn>: the Price family members are psychologically specific, driven by clearly articulated objectives and given circumstances. Their naturalistic dialogue scenes demand the kind of truthful, interior-driven performance that Stanislavski's system develops.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n10">Frantic Assembly's movement sequences, however, operate in a different register. They are not naturalistic: a character does not lift their sibling in a stylised arc because that is what people literally do when they argue. The movement abstracts the emotional truth. The hybrid form of the play &mdash; Stanislavskian naturalism interrupted by Frantic Assembly physicality &mdash; means that performers must be trained in both modes and must be able to shift between them convincingly within a single scene. This is a significant practical demand and a rich source of exam questions about practitioner application.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Adelaide, 2016: The Contemporary Australian Setting</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The play is set in contemporary Adelaide, South Australia. Adelaide is a city often associated in Australian cultural imagination with suburban respectability, middle-class aspiration and a certain quiet conservatism &mdash; a city where people know what is expected of them. Bovell uses this <dfn class="term" data-def="The social, historical and cultural circumstances that surround the creation of a play and that shape how audiences understand it.">context</dfn> to make the Price family's story both specific (Australian, contemporary, suburban) and universal (family secrets, generational conflict, the distance between who we are and who others expect us to be).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">The play premiered at the State Theatre Company South Australia in May 2016, the first performance in Adelaide. It then transferred later that year to the UK in a Frantic Assembly co-production at the Lyric Hammersmith in London, directed by Geordie Brookman and Scott Graham, followed by a UK tour. The British audience encountered the play as both a foreign text (Australian voices, Australian suburban landscape) and a recognisable one: the themes of family, identity and parental love cross cultural contexts. For an exam answer about context, this dual reception is significant: Bovell writes from inside Australian suburbia but with full awareness that the play will be received internationally.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name the city of the world premiere, the year, and the UK venue where the play transferred.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>World premiere: State Theatre Company South Australia, Adelaide, May 2016. UK premiere: Lyric Hammersmith / Frantic Assembly co-production, 2016, directed by Geordie Brookman and Scott Graham. The play sits within Australian suburban-realist theatre but is shaped by British physical theatre practice.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Context in the Design Room: What 2016 Adelaide Means Practically</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Context shapes design choices. A production setting the play in its original Adelaide context might use warm, sun-bleached costume colours for the parents &mdash; indicating outdoor suburban life &mdash; while giving the returning children clothes that suggest their distance from home (Rosie in Berlin-influenced layering, Ben in corporate wear that reads as slightly out of place in the back garden). The <dfn class="term" data-def="The physical items worn by performers in a production, including clothing, shoes, hair, make-up and accessories, designed to communicate character, status and period.">costume design</dfn> can communicate, without a word, where each character has come from and how far they have travelled from their parents' world.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n17">The garden itself, in design terms, is the single most important set element for communicating context. A designer using real or artificial rose plants, a warm earth-coloured floor cloth, and a kitchen visible behind a glass or gauze partition is immediately communicating Australian suburban domesticity. Lighting in this setting tends towards warm ambers in the garden during daytime scenes and cooler, more interior tones in the kitchen &mdash; a <dfn class="term" data-def="Lighting that covers a broad area of the stage with even, relatively undifferentiated light.">lighting wash</dfn> that shifts in quality between the two spaces to reinforce their symbolic difference.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: Frantic Assembly Sequences Are Optional</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Students sometimes treat the movement sequences as an interesting add-on to an otherwise straight naturalistic play. This fundamentally misunderstands the co-development process. Bovell and Graham built the movement sequences into the structure of the play: they occur at moments where the emotional content exceeds what naturalistic dialogue can carry. Removing them would remove some of the play's most significant meaning-making moments. In any answer about the play's form and style, students should describe it as a hybrid form: naturalistic family drama with integrated physical theatre sequences, rather than naturalism with optional dance interludes.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n21">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul><li data-narration-id="n22">Andrew Bovell (born 1962) is an Australian playwright whose work consistently explores family secrets and the destabilising effect of revealed truth; the Nick Hern Modern Plays edition is the prescribed text for AQA.</li><li data-narration-id="n23">Frantic Assembly co-developed the play with Bovell: the movement sequences are structural, not decorative, and require performers trained in both Stanislavskian naturalism and physical theatre.</li><li data-narration-id="n24">The contemporary Adelaide setting gives designers a specific visual and cultural language (suburban warmth, garden, domestic kitchen) and makes the play's themes both locally specific and internationally universal.</li></ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">For the 12-mark question about playwright intentions and context, the most common error is treating context as background decoration (&ldquo;the play is set in Australia so it has Australian characters&rdquo;). Strong answers use context analytically: explain what the suburban Adelaide setting allows Bovell to do (set up middle-class expectations that each revelation then undercuts); explain what the Frantic Assembly co-development means for the play's form (movement sequences are structural, not decorative); and explain how the 2016 premiere context &mdash; a contemporary Australian audience, followed by a British one &mdash; shapes how the themes are received.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n20">For the 20-mark extended staging response, if the question focuses on design for a specific context-related moment, demonstrate your knowledge of the co-production: show that you understand Frantic Assembly's vocabulary (chair duets, contact lifts) and Stanislavski's system (given circumstances, objective), and that you can apply both to a named moment in the play. A strong answer will acknowledge the hybrid nature of the form and make different design choices for the naturalistic sections and the movement sequences.</p>

