=== L5 Dramatic Methods: Monologue and Movement ===
id: b58ebd27-0489-43c2-9622-7778437f3891
slug: dramatic-methods-monologue-and-movement
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:12:49.66545+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How Bovell weaves naturalistic dialogue with direct-address monologues, and how movement carries subtext.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Two Languages in One Play</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Andrew Bovell gives <em>Things I Know to Be True</em> two distinct theatrical languages that work in counterpoint throughout the play. The first is naturalistic family dialogue &mdash; the everyday speech of a suburban household, full of interruptions, silences and unfinished thoughts. The second is a more openly theatrical language: <dfn class="term" data-def="A speech in which a character addresses the audience directly, stepping outside the action of the scene and breaking the illusion of a fourth wall.">direct address</dfn> monologue and stylised physical movement. Understanding how Bovell moves between these two registers &mdash; and what that movement achieves for the audience &mdash; is central to answering any question about dramatic methods in this play.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">The alternation is structural. Naturalistic scenes establish the domestic reality of the Price family: their relationships, their habits, the particular tensions of a household where four adult children are still emotionally tied to parents who are themselves imperfect. Then, at moments of heightened emotion, the register shifts. A character steps outside the scene to speak directly to the audience, or the family moves into a Frantic Assembly movement sequence that says in bodies what cannot be said in words.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, explain in two sentences the difference between a naturalistic dialogue scene and a direct-address monologue in this play.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Bovell uses two theatrical registers: naturalistic dialogue (characters speak to each other as real people might) and direct address (a character speaks directly to the audience, stepping outside the scene). Moving between these registers is itself a dramatic method.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Rosie and the Art of Direct Address</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Rosie functions as the play&rsquo;s primary narrator-figure. Her <dfn class="term" data-def="A speech delivered by one performer alone, either to the audience or as a form of private reflection made public.">monologue</dfn> passages are spoken from a temporal position that is partly outside the action: she often knows, or half-knows, what the season will bring. This creates a layer of <dfn class="term" data-def="A dramatic device in which the audience possesses information that one or more characters on stage do not yet have.">dramatic irony</dfn> &mdash; the audience senses things will go wrong before they do, sharing a partial foreknowledge with Rosie that the other characters do not have.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">In production, the staging of Rosie&rsquo;s direct-address passages must communicate her dual temporal position. A director might place her at <dfn class="term" data-def="The area of the stage furthest from the audience, away from the front edge.">upstage</dfn> centre while the family activity continues <dfn class="term" data-def="The area of the stage closest to the audience, at the front edge.">downstage</dfn>, visually separating her narrator-self from her character-self. A lighting designer might use a tight <dfn class="term" data-def="A focused beam of light directed at a specific performer or area of the stage, isolating them from their surroundings.">lighting special</dfn> to pull her out of the general <dfn class="term" data-def="A broad, even distribution of light across the whole stage or a large section of it.">lighting wash</dfn>, signalling to the audience that she is speaking from a different register of truth.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: Monologues Are Just Narration</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">Students sometimes treat Rosie&rsquo;s direct-address passages as straightforward narration &mdash; a way of telling the audience what is happening. This misreads their function. Rosie&rsquo;s monologues carry subtext: they express what she feels but cannot say to the family, and they position the audience as her confidant rather than a detached observer. As a performer playing Rosie, you are not reporting; you are sharing something that costs you emotionally. The vocal and physical choices must reflect that emotional cost. Speaking at a steady, measured pace with a slightly lower volume than the dialogue scenes suggests intimacy; allowing brief pauses before key revelations gives the audience space to feel the weight of what is coming.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Movement as Subtext: The Frantic Assembly Sequences</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">The physical theatre sequences in <em>Things I Know to Be True</em> were developed in collaboration with Frantic Assembly, the British physical theatre company co-directed by Scott Graham. Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s approach to movement is rooted in everyday physicality rather than stylised dance: they build sequences from contact improvisation, counter-balance, and the reordering of familiar gestures. The result is movement that feels emotionally urgent rather than aesthetically decorative.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">In this play, the movement sequences function as heightened <dfn class="term" data-def="The implied or unstated meaning beneath the surface dialogue or action of a scene; what characters feel but do not say.">subtext</dfn>. When the Price family&rsquo;s emotional life exceeds what their naturalistic dialogue can hold, Bovell&rsquo;s stage directions call for a physical sequence. These moments might involve a lift that expresses both love and the effort of holding a relationship together, a <dfn class="term" data-def="A Frantic Assembly technique in which two performers work with chairs as contact-improvisation props, creating lifts, slides and counter-balance sequences.">chair duet</dfn> that externalises a conversation neither character can have in words, or the round-by-through-under contact work in which bodies pass through each other&rsquo;s space, suggesting emotional entanglement.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Without looking, write down two things that a Frantic Assembly movement sequence communicates that naturalistic dialogue alone cannot.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Frantic Assembly movement sequences in the play externalise subtext: the things characters feel but cannot verbalise. A lift might express the effort of parental love; a chair duet might show the negotiation inside a relationship. The movement is not illustrative &mdash; it reveals.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">The Garden Monologue and Rose Imagery</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Bob&rsquo;s rose garden is not simply a set piece; it is a recurring motif that carries a great deal of the play&rsquo;s emotional weight without being stated directly. When Bob speaks in or near the garden &mdash; tending, pruning, simply being &mdash; the garden becomes the physical correlative of his inner life: ordered, carefully maintained, beautiful in a way that the rest of his world is not. In staging terms, a sound designer might build a subtle <dfn class="term" data-def="A continuous layer of ambient sound constructed from multiple recorded or live elements to create atmosphere or environment.">soundscape</dfn> for the garden: birdsong, a light breeze, the distant sound of cicadas in summer &mdash; sounds that contrast with the harder, closer acoustic of the kitchen interior.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The rose imagery also works in conjunction with <dfn class="term" data-def="Mood and atmosphere created through all the theatrical elements working together to produce a specific emotional climate in the audience.">mood and atmosphere</dfn>. In spring, the roses are beginning: fresh colour, the optimism of new growth. By winter, bare stems. A designer who makes the rose bush a visible element of the set can track this seasonal arc visually, so that the garden itself becomes a kind of scenic narrator &mdash; telling the audience where in the emotional year they are without any dialogue needing to do that work.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Performer and Designer Approaches to Monologue Moments</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">As a <strong>performer</strong> delivering a direct-address monologue, you face a specific challenge: you are speaking to the audience, not to another character, so the usual triggers of reaction and response are removed. To hold this, you must anchor each moment in a specific thought or feeling. Vocally, you might use a slower pace than the dialogue scenes, a slightly raised head so that eye-line reaches out across the auditorium, and variation in volume &mdash; dropping to near-quiet for the most privately held thoughts. Physically, stillness is a powerful choice: if the rest of the production is in motion, a still figure speaking directly out commands attention.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n17">As a <strong>lighting designer</strong>, a direct-address monologue offers the chance to make a distinct visual statement. Removing the character from the general wash and placing them in a tight profile spot &mdash; a warm amber or a cooler neutral depending on the season &mdash; signals the change in register without any spoken announcement. If the family remains dimly visible upstage in a reduced wash while Rosie speaks downstage in a special, the audience holds two simultaneous spaces: the world of the family and the world of the narrator. This dual focus is one of the most effective staging solutions for the monologue moments.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul><li data-narration-id="n21">Bovell writes in two theatrical registers: naturalistic dialogue and direct-address monologue / stylised movement. The shift between them is itself a dramatic method, signalling to the audience that the play is moving into a different kind of truth.</li><li data-narration-id="n22">Rosie&rsquo;s direct-address monologues create dramatic irony: she speaks from partial foreknowledge, making the audience share a sense of what is coming before it arrives. As a performer, this demands holding two timeframes in the body simultaneously.</li><li data-narration-id="n23">Frantic Assembly movement sequences externalise subtext &mdash; the emotional content that dialogue cannot contain. The rose garden&rsquo;s soundscape and lighting state can reinforce the seasonal arc, giving the play a visual and auditory narrative that runs in parallel to the spoken one.</li></ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">For the 12-mark question on playwright&rsquo;s intentions, a common error is to treat direct address and movement sequences as separate topics rather than analysing them as two parts of the same dramatic method: both are ways Bovell controls the audience&rsquo;s relationship to the action. Ask yourself: what does Bovell want the audience to feel at the moment Rosie steps outside the scene? What does he want them to feel during a movement sequence? Your answer must address the intended effect on the audience, not just describe the technique.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">For the 20-mark extended staging response, do not separate performer and designer as though they belong to different questions. The strongest answers show how a performer&rsquo;s stillness during direct address is amplified by a lighting special, or how a movement sequence gains meaning because the <dfn class="term" data-def="A composition of ambient and composed sound used to create a specific environment or emotional atmosphere.">soundscape</dfn> shifts to something non-diegetic and abstract. Every staging choice in a movement sequence should be justified with reference to the specific emotional moment it represents.</p>

