=== L7 Performance Interpretation: Bob's Final Tableau ===
id: 4344b866-8601-4075-aaad-a020ccfd268a
slug: performance-interpretation-bobs-final-tableau
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:12:52.155764+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Vocal and physical choices for Bob, Fran and the four children in the play's most charged moments.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Performing a Family Under Pressure</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The Price family in <em>Things I Know to Be True</em> are ordinary people in an extraordinary year. Each character carries a different kind of pressure, and a performer&rsquo;s job is to hold that pressure truthfully across the entire arc of the play &mdash; not only in the big revelatory scenes but in the domestic moments in between. Andrew Bovell&rsquo;s naturalistic dialogue gives performers rich material: overlapping thoughts, sentences that stop before they finish, the particular shorthand of a family that has been talking to each other for decades. Understanding how to use <dfn class="term" data-def="The qualities of a performer&rsquo;s voice in performance: volume, pitch, pace, pause, tone and emphasis.">vocal interpretation</dfn> and <dfn class="term" data-def="How a performer uses their body in performance: gesture, posture, facial expression, spatial positioning and movement.">physical interpretation</dfn> for each character is the foundation of every performance answer in the exam.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">This lesson focuses on Bob, Fran, and the four children at the play&rsquo;s most charged moments: Bob&rsquo;s stoicism in the face of his children&rsquo;s revelations; Fran&rsquo;s directness and toughness; Mark&rsquo;s reveal scene; Pip&rsquo;s departure; and the ensemble physicality of the play&rsquo;s ending tableau. In each case, the performer must balance what the character says with what the character cannot say &mdash; the <dfn class="term" data-def="The implied or unstated meaning beneath the surface dialogue; what a character feels but does not express in words.">subtext</dfn> that Bovell builds into every scene.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name two specific vocal choices and two specific physical choices a performer could make to show a character who is holding back strong emotion.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>For every performance question, you must name both vocal AND physical choices, anchored to a specific named moment. Saying &ldquo;I would act sad&rdquo; is not enough. Name the tool: slower pace, a drop in volume, a held pause; or a stillness in the body, a lowered gaze, hands released to the sides. The more specific you are, the higher the mark.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Bob: Stoicism and the Weight of What Is Unsaid</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Bob Price is a study in restraint. He tends his roses, maintains a sense of order, and responds to each of his children&rsquo;s revelations with a quietness that the audience must learn to read. His silence is not emptiness; it is full of suppressed feeling. A performer playing Bob must understand this as the central technical challenge of the role: how to communicate emotional depth through stillness and economy rather than through display.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">In the garden scenes, Bob&rsquo;s physicality can be rooted in a specific task &mdash; he is pruning, or turning soil, or standing with his hands at his sides among the plants. <dfn class="term" data-def="The physical distance and spatial relationship between performers on stage, used to communicate status, relationship and emotional state.">Proxemics</dfn> matter enormously for this character: when a child approaches him in the garden, the way Bob adjusts his position &mdash; whether he turns fully to face them, or continues his task with his body angled slightly away, or holds himself very still &mdash; communicates volumes before a word is spoken. Vocally, Bob speaks measured, unhurried. A slower pace than the other characters; pauses before significant responses; a volume that does not rise even under pressure. When he receives a revelation that shocks him, the most powerful choice is often to lower the volume still further: a near-whisper that forces the audience to lean in.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Performer Worked Example: Mark&rsquo;s Reveal Scene</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">The summer scene in which Mark tells his parents that he is transitioning to Mia is one of the play&rsquo;s most complex performance challenges. For the performer playing Mark, the approach must be grounded in the character&rsquo;s specific circumstances: this is not a generalised coming-out scene but a particular person, in a particular family, telling two particular parents something that has cost him an enormous amount to hold back. The given circumstances &mdash; who Bob is, who Fran is, how the family communicates &mdash; shape every choice.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n9">Vocally, the performer playing Mark might begin the scene with a pace that is slightly faster than usual &mdash; the energy of something that must be said before the courage runs out &mdash; and then slow significantly once the words are out, allowing the silence of the parents&rsquo; response to land. Physically, Mark might begin the scene at a measured distance from his parents &mdash; neither close nor far, a neutral proxemics &mdash; and during the most difficult part of the speech take a step towards them rather than away, communicating that the revelation is an act of trust rather than an exit. A small, specific gesture &mdash; the hands open, facing upward, at the sides, as if offering something &mdash; can communicate vulnerability without overplaying the emotional register. Examiners reward specificity: not &ldquo;I would look scared&rdquo; but &ldquo;I would hold my body very still from the waist down while allowing a slight tremor in the hands, so that the effort of physical control is visible to the audience.&rdquo;</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Fran: Directness Under Pressure</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Where Bob retreats into stillness, Fran advances. She is the more direct of the two parents &mdash; a nurse by profession, accustomed to saying difficult things plainly. A performer playing Fran must resist the temptation to play her as simply hard. Her directness is a form of love: she speaks truth because she believes her children deserve it. The challenge is to hold both qualities in the body at once: the toughness and the vulnerability beneath it.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">In the kitchen confrontation scenes, Fran&rsquo;s physicality is active: she might be at the kitchen counter or the table, engaged in a practical task, and she continues that task for longer than is comfortable while a conversation is beginning around her. This use of physical business &mdash; the act of doing something with the hands while a difficult emotional conversation unfolds &mdash; is a classic <dfn class="term" data-def="Acting technique in which an actor uses personal experience and emotional memory to generate truthful responses in a role.">Stanislavskian</dfn> approach to grounding a scene in physical reality. When Fran finally stops and turns to face the person she is speaking with, the stillness of that turn communicates that something significant is now happening.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Without looking, write one sentence describing a specific physical choice for Fran in a kitchen confrontation scene and explain what it communicates to the audience.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Fran&rsquo;s directness is grounded in physical business: she works with her hands while conversations unfold around her, and the moment she stops signals to the audience that something real is now being said. As a performer, using and then releasing physical business is a precise technique for controlling the rhythm of a scene.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">The Ensemble and the Ending Tableau</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The play&rsquo;s final image is a <dfn class="term" data-def="A still image created by performers holding positions, used to encapsulate a moment of significance or to frame the end of a scene.">tableau</dfn> &mdash; the whole family gathered in a configuration that carries the weight of everything the year has delivered. In the Frantic Assembly co-production tradition, this moment may be embedded within or arrived at through a movement sequence: the family assembling themselves physically, finding their spatial relationship to each other and to Bob at the centre, so that the final image is not simply &ldquo;they stand together&rdquo; but a precisely constructed statement about love, damage, truth and endurance.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">As a director working with the ensemble for this moment, the key decisions are about <dfn class="term" data-def="The arrangement and movement of performers on stage, determined by the director to create meaning through position and spatial relationships.">blocking</dfn> and spatial hierarchy. Who is closest to Bob? Who is furthest away? Who makes physical contact with another character, and who is isolated? Does the tableau face the audience directly, or is it angled so that some characters have their backs to us, creating a sense of privacy or enclosure? Each of these decisions changes the meaning of the final image and must be justified in relation to the revelations the play has delivered across the year. The lighting state for the final tableau should be considered: a slow fade rather than a blackout, perhaps, so that the audience holds the image for longer &mdash; both literally and emotionally.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Error: Playing the Emotion, Not the Thought</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">A common exam error in performance-interpretation answers is to describe what the character is feeling (&ldquo;I would show that Bob is devastated&rdquo;) rather than describing the specific vocal and physical choices that communicate that feeling without naming it. Audiences do not need to be told what a character feels; they need to be shown it through concrete theatrical means. Think of this as the difference between illustrating an emotion and communicating a thought. The character is not &ldquo;performing sadness&rdquo;; the character is pursuing a specific action (trying to hold themselves together, trying not to let a child see the damage their words have done), and it is that pursuit &mdash; the effort of it &mdash; that the audience reads as feeling. Always ask: what is this character <em>doing</em>, physically and vocally, in this moment? Start there, not with the emotion label.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul><li data-narration-id="n21">Every performance answer requires specific vocal AND physical choices anchored to a named moment. Naming the emotion is not enough; name the technique: volume, pace, pause, pitch; posture, gesture, proxemics, stillness.</li><li data-narration-id="n22">Bob&rsquo;s stoicism is communicated through restraint &mdash; a slower pace, near-whisper volume under pressure, stillness in the body; Fran&rsquo;s directness through physical business that she then releases to signal importance. Both characters require technical precision rather than emotional display.</li><li data-narration-id="n23">The ending tableau is an ensemble staging question: blocking, spatial hierarchy, physical contact, and the lighting state that holds the final image must all be considered together. The Frantic Assembly tradition of arriving at stillness through movement gives this moment particular theatrical weight.</li></ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">For the 8-mark &lsquo;Interpret as Performer&rsquo; question, you must give both a vocal AND a physical choice, each anchored to a named moment of the play. A common error is to write only about how the character feels without specifying the vocal or physical technique that communicates that feeling. The mark scheme requires: a specific vocal choice (e.g. &ldquo;I would drop my volume to a near-whisper as Bob receives Mark&rsquo;s news, slowing my pace to one beat per word so that each word lands separately&rdquo;) AND a specific physical choice (e.g. &ldquo;I would hold my body absolutely still, feet planted, hands released to my sides, allowing only the smallest shift of weight forward to communicate that I am listening with my whole self&rdquo;). Name the moment, name the tools, explain the effect on the audience.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">For the 20-mark extended staging response, an answer focused on the ending tableau must address both performer choices for named characters AND at least one design discipline. The tableau is not just about position; it is about light (what lighting state holds the final image?), sound (is there underscore as the tableau forms, or silence?) and the specific body language of each character in relation to the others. Think spatially as well as dramatically.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
