=== L8 Practitioner Application: Stanislavski Meets Frantic Assembly ===
id: 38e9e21b-1d55-4084-b4ee-8b821e0a69f7
slug: practitioner-application-stanislavski-meets-frantic-assembly
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-19T11:13:00.044978+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How naturalistic acting and physical-theatre movement coexist in Bovell's hybrid play.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">A Hybrid Form</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><em>Things I Know to Be True</em> is one of very few GCSE set plays that is genuinely hybrid in its theatrical form: it was written to be performed using two distinct and historically separate performance traditions in the same production. The naturalistic family scenes draw on <dfn class="term" data-def="A theatrical style pioneered by Konstantin Stanislavski that seeks truthful, internally motivated performance, grounding character in specific given circumstances and emotional authenticity.">Stanislavskian acting</dfn> principles. The movement sequences were developed in collaboration with Frantic Assembly and draw on <dfn class="term" data-def="A form of theatre in which movement and physical expression are as important as, or more important than, spoken text, often using contact improvisation, ensemble dynamics and non-naturalistic gesture.">physical theatre</dfn> practice. Understanding both traditions and &mdash; crucially &mdash; how they interact in this play is the foundation of the practitioner-application question for this unit.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">This is not a case of two traditions accidentally colliding. Bovell wrote the play specifically with Scott Graham of Frantic Assembly as a creative partner; the movement sequences are not added after the fact but are built into the dramatic structure. The result is a form where the transitions between registers &mdash; from dialogue to movement, from naturalism to physical abstraction &mdash; carry meaning in themselves. Understanding when and why those transitions happen is as important as understanding each tradition in isolation.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, explain in two sentences what makes Things I Know to Be True a 'hybrid' theatrical form and why that matters for exam answers.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Things I Know to Be True uses two traditions deliberately: Stanislavskian emotional truth for the naturalistic dialogue scenes, and Frantic Assembly physical theatre for the movement sequences. Both are structural, not decorative. A student who treats the movement as &lsquo;extra&rsquo; misses half the play&rsquo;s theatrical language.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Stanislavski and the Price Family</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Konstantin Stanislavski (1863&ndash;1938) developed a systematic approach to actor training that changed the way realistic drama is performed. His central insight was that truthful acting comes from the actor understanding the character&rsquo;s inner life: their history, desires, relationships, and the specific circumstances of the scene. He called this the <dfn class="term" data-def="Stanislavski&rsquo;s term for all the information about a character&rsquo;s situation: who they are, where they are, when the scene takes place, and what has happened before.">given circumstances</dfn>. For a performer playing any member of the Price family, the given circumstances are richly specified by Bovell: each character&rsquo;s profession, their history with each other, the year they are having, the specific weight they carry into each scene.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Stanislavski also asked actors to pursue an <dfn class="term" data-def="Stanislavski&rsquo;s term for the overall goal a character is pursuing throughout the entire play; the driving desire that shapes all their actions.">objective</dfn> in every scene &mdash; a specific, playable goal that the character is trying to achieve. For Bob in the garden scenes, his objective might be simply &ldquo;to keep this world intact&rdquo; &mdash; to maintain the order and beauty of the space as a counterweight to the disorder his children keep introducing. For Fran in the kitchen confrontations, her objective might be &ldquo;to make my child tell me the truth&rdquo; or &ldquo;to protect this family from what is coming.&rdquo; When a performer anchors their choices to a clear objective, every physical and vocal decision becomes purposeful: it is always in the service of getting what the character wants.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Stanislavski&rsquo;s Given Circumstances Applied to Mark / Mia</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">For the performer playing Mark, whose arc moves towards his identity as Mia, Stanislavski&rsquo;s given circumstances are particularly important. The examiner rewards sensitive, specific choices that are grounded in who this character is in relation to this family. The given circumstances include: Mark&rsquo;s relationship with his parents (the particular emotional dynamic of each parent); his sense of what the revelation will mean for the family; the specific setting (the kitchen or the garden, indoors or out, the season); and the circumstances that immediately precede the scene. A Stanislavskian performer does not play a generalised &ldquo;coming out scene&rdquo; &mdash; they play this character, in this moment, with these specific people. The choices that come from that specificity are always more compelling, and more mark-worthy, than the generic ones.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Frantic Assembly: Physical Theatre as Emotional Language</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Frantic Assembly was founded in 1994 by Scott Graham, Steven Hoggett and Vicki Middleton. The company&rsquo;s physical theatre practice is distinctive in that it is not primarily dance: it grows from everyday movement &mdash; the physicality of ordinary people &mdash; and heightens it into something expressive. Their key techniques include <dfn class="term" data-def="A Frantic Assembly movement technique in which two or more performers pass through each other&rsquo;s bodies in a continuous flowing sequence, creating a sense of fluidity and emotional entanglement.">round-by-through-under</dfn> sequences, contact lifts, and chair duets. Scott Graham was the co-director of the original production with Geordie Brookman, contributing movement direction specifically shaped around the Price family&rsquo;s emotional dynamics.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">What makes Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s approach relevant to <em>Things I Know to Be True</em> specifically is their philosophical starting point: movement should communicate what words cannot. In the context of this play, this translates directly to the moments of greatest emotional intensity. When Bob and a child cannot speak to each other directly about what is happening, Bovell gives them a movement sequence instead. The sequence does not illustrate the conversation; it replaces it with something more honest. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A Frantic Assembly technique in which two performers use chairs as contact-improvisation props to create a sequence of lifts, slides, counter-balances and shared weight.">chair duet</dfn> between Bob and Rosie, for instance, might communicate across several minutes of movement everything that a two-page conversation between them could not.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name two Frantic Assembly physical theatre techniques and explain what each one communicates in the context of a family drama.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s key techniques in this play: chair duets (two performers using chairs as contact-improvisation props, expressing relationship through shared weight and negotiated movement); lifts (one performer carrying or lifting another, expressing both protection and the effort of love); and round-by-through-under sequences (continuous, flowing movement through each other&rsquo;s bodies, suggesting emotional entanglement). None of these are decorative; all are structurally placed to replace dialogue at the point where dialogue fails.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">The Moment of Transition: Moving Between Registers</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">The most interesting theatrical moments in <em>Things I Know to Be True</em> are the transitions: the point where naturalistic dialogue ends and a movement sequence begins, or where a movement sequence dissolves back into dialogue. How does a company signal to the audience that the register is changing? And how does a designer support those transitions?</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">In production, these transitions are typically managed through a combination of sound, lighting and the performers&rsquo; own physical approach to stillness. The dialogue scene reaches its emotional peak &mdash; a phrase, a silence, a look &mdash; and then the movement begins, often initiated by a single performer before the others join. A sound designer might cue a shift in the <dfn class="term" data-def="A continuous ambient sound environment constructed from multiple elements, used to create place and emotional atmosphere.">soundscape</dfn>: the domestic acoustic of the kitchen falls away and a non-diegetic, texturally abstract underscore takes its place. A lighting designer might allow the <dfn class="term" data-def="The quality, colour and intensity of light on stage at a given moment, created by the combination of all active lanterns.">lighting state</dfn> to shift slightly: the movement sequences often benefit from a reduction in overall intensity, with one or two moving lights following the performers, so that the space feels different &mdash; more interior, more subjective &mdash; than it does during the dialogue scenes. When the movement ends and dialogue resumes, the lighting returns, the soundscape resolves, and the audience is brought back to the domestic world they were in before.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Practitioner Comparison: Stanislavski and Frantic Assembly Side by Side</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">Stanislavski&rsquo;s system asks: what does this character want? What are their given circumstances? How does their past shape this moment? It starts from the inside &mdash; from the character&rsquo;s inner life &mdash; and works outward to the physical expression of that inner life. The result, when it works, is a performance that appears effortlessly truthful: the words and the body seem to arise from the same source.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n17">Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s approach asks: what does this body do? How do two bodies relate to each other in space? What does shared weight communicate that words cannot? It starts from the outside &mdash; from physical contact and spatial relationship &mdash; and works inward. The performers are not asked to &ldquo;feel something and show it&rdquo;; they are asked to execute a precise physical task, and the feeling arises from the task. This is in some ways the inverse of Stanislavski&rsquo;s method, yet in <em>Things I Know to Be True</em> the two approaches are placed alongside each other in the same production and the same family, and they reinforce rather than contradict each other. The Stanislavskian given circumstances of the Price family give the Frantic Assembly movement sequences their specific emotional charge; the movement sequences, in turn, reveal the subterranean emotional life that Stanislavskian truth keeps below the surface of the dialogue.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul><li data-narration-id="n21">Things I Know to Be True uses two traditions in deliberate combination: Stanislavskian acting for the naturalistic dialogue scenes (given circumstances, objectives, emotional truth) and Frantic Assembly physical theatre for the movement sequences (chair duets, lifts, round-by-through-under). Both are built into the structure, not added as decoration.</li><li data-narration-id="n22">Stanislavski starts from the inside (inner life, given circumstances) and works outward; Frantic Assembly starts from the outside (physical task, shared weight) and works inward. In this play, the two approaches reinforce each other: Stanislavskian truth charges the movement with specific emotional meaning; the movement reveals what the dialogue keeps below the surface.</li><li data-narration-id="n23">The transitions between registers &mdash; dialogue to movement, movement back to dialogue &mdash; are supported by sound and lighting design. A soundscape shift and a change in lighting state signal to the audience that the register has changed. Every transition is a design opportunity.</li></ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">For any question that asks you to apply practitioner techniques to this play, you must do more than describe the practitioner&rsquo;s biography. Examiners are looking for the technique applied to a specific moment of the set play. The pattern to follow is: name the practitioner, name the technique, name the moment in the play where it applies, and explain what the technique achieves for the audience at that moment. For example: &ldquo;Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s chair-duet technique could be applied to the moment when Bob and a child are unable to speak directly to each other &mdash; the shared weight and negotiated movement of the duet communicates the effort of their relationship without any dialogue needing to name it, creating an emotional honesty the naturalistic scene cannot reach.&rdquo;</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">For the 20-mark extended staging response, a question about practitioner application is also a staging question: you must address how the technique manifests in performance AND how a designer supports it. The movement sequence and the lighting state that accompanies it are part of the same theatrical statement. Do not describe one without the other.</p>

