"C:\\WINDOWS\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe" -Command '$file = Join-Path (Get-Location) '"'scripts/_retrofc/units/drama-aqa__things-i-know-to-be-true/_edits.json'; "'$edits=[System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($file,[System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8)|ConvertFrom-Json; $edits | Where-Object {$_.lesson -eq 4} | ForEach-Object {$_.find}' in C:\Users\tshau\Documents\Study Vault
 succeeded in 298ms:
<p data-narration-id="n19">For the 12-mark question about playwright intentions and context, the most common error is treating context as background decoration (&ldquo;the play is set in Australia so it has Australian characters&rdquo;). Strong answers use context analytically: explain what the suburban Adelaide setting allows Bovell to do (set up middle-class expectations that each revelation then undercuts); explain what the Frantic Assembly co-development means for the play&rsquo;s form (movement sequences are structural, not decorative); and explain how the 2016 premiere context &mdash; a contemporary Australian audience, followed by a British one &mdash; shapes how the themes are received.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n20">For the 20-mark extended staging response, if the question focuses on design for a specific context-related moment, demonstrate your knowledge of the co-production: show that you understand Frantic Assembly&rsquo;s vocabulary (chair duets, contact lifts) and Stanislavski&rsquo;s system (given circumstances, objective), and that you can apply both to a named moment in the play. A strong answer will acknowledge the hybrid nature of the form and make different design choices for the naturalistic sections and the movement sequences.</p>

