@@ -0,0 +1,141 @@
+[
+  {
+    "lesson": 1,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">For the 12-mark question asking you to analyse playwright intentions, the most common error is describing what happens in each season rather than explaining <em>why</em> Bovell chose to structure the play this way. Ask yourself: what does the seasonal frame allow Bovell to do that a conventional act structure would not? How does the accumulation of revelations create a specific effect on the audience by the time the final shock lands? Your answer must refer to what the playwright wants the audience to think or feel at that moment &mdash; not just what the characters do.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">When analysing the play&rsquo;s structure in practice, avoid simply retelling events. Explain how a structural choice shapes the audience&rsquo;s response. Ask what the seasonal frame, the accumulation of revelations or a shift in theatrical register allows Bovell to communicate.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F3: removes the inaccurate board-question claim."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 1,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n19\">For the 20-mark extended staging response, remember you must address both performer and designer choices. A strong answer for a plot-and-structure question might discuss: the lighting state transitions between seasons (designer), the use of upstage/downstage space for the garden and kitchen (director/set designer), and the vocal register a performer uses in a direct-address monologue compared to a naturalistic dialogue scene (performer). All three must be anchored to specific named moments in the play.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n19\">For an extended response, use the performer or designer perspective named in the question. Keep every choice specific, link it to a moment in the play and explain its intended effect on the audience.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F4: removes the false requirement to cover both perspectives."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n21\">For the 12-mark question asking you to analyse playwright intentions on a moment related to theme, the most common error is describing what happens rather than explaining what Bovell wants the audience to feel or understand at that moment. Ask yourself: is Bovell testing the limits of unconditional love here? Is he staging the gap between private truth and public performance? What specific dramatic method (staging, structure, character interaction) is he using to make the theme visible, and what effect should that method have on the audience?</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n21\">When analysing a thematic moment in practice, avoid simply describing events. Explain what the dramatic methods make the audience think or feel. Ask how staging, structure or character interaction makes the theme visible.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F7: removes the inaccurate board-question claim."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 3,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n22\">For the 20-mark staging response, if the question asks you to stage a key thematic moment, your answer must include both performer choices (vocal and physical) and at least one design discipline. For example, a question about the theme of identity and the Mark / Mia revelation demands: a specific lighting choice to stage the isolation and inclusion described above; a specific costume decision (what does Mia wear when she arrives, and how does that contrast with earlier scenes?); and specific performer choices for Fran and Bob (how does each parent physically respond to their child before they speak?).</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n22\">For an extended response, use the performer or designer perspective named in the question. Give detailed choices relevant to that role, link them to the extract and the whole play, and explain their intended effects.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F8: removes the false requirement to combine performer and design responses."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 4,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n19\">For the 12-mark question about playwright intentions and context, the most common error is treating context as background decoration (&ldquo;the play is set in Australia so it has Australian characters&rdquo;). Strong answers use context analytically: explain what the suburban Adelaide setting allows Bovell to do (set up middle-class expectations that each revelation then undercuts); explain what the Frantic Assembly co-development means for the play's form (movement sequences are structural, not decorative); and explain how the 2016 premiere context &mdash; a contemporary Australian audience, followed by a British one &mdash; shapes how the themes are received.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n19\">When discussing context in practice, use it analytically rather than as background decoration. Explain how the Adelaide setting or Frantic Assembly collaboration can shape a production choice and its effect on an audience.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F9: removes the inaccurate board-question claim."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 4,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n20\">For the 20-mark extended staging response, if the question focuses on design for a specific context-related moment, demonstrate your knowledge of the co-production: show that you understand Frantic Assembly's vocabulary (chair duets, contact lifts) and Stanislavski's system (given circumstances, objective), and that you can apply both to a named moment in the play. A strong answer will acknowledge the hybrid nature of the form and make different design choices for the naturalistic sections and the movement sequences.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n20\">For an extended response, use the performer or designer perspective named in the question. Apply relevant contextual knowledge to a specific moment and explain the intended effect on the audience.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F10: removes the false combined-response requirement."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">For the 12-mark question on playwright&rsquo;s intentions, a common error is to treat direct address and movement sequences as separate topics rather than analysing them as two parts of the same dramatic method: both are ways Bovell controls the audience&rsquo;s relationship to the action. Ask yourself: what does Bovell want the audience to feel at the moment Rosie steps outside the scene? What does he want them to feel during a movement sequence? Your answer must address the intended effect on the audience, not just describe the technique.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">When analysing direct address and movement in practice, consider how each controls the audience&rsquo;s relationship to the action. Explain the intended effect on the audience as well as describing the technique.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F12: removes the inaccurate board-question claim."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 5,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n19\">For the 20-mark extended staging response, do not separate performer and designer as though they belong to different questions. The strongest answers show how a performer&rsquo;s stillness during direct address is amplified by a lighting special, or how a movement sequence gains meaning because the <dfn class=\"term\" data-def=\"A composition of ambient and composed sound used to create a specific environment or emotional atmosphere.\">soundscape</dfn> shifts to something non-diegetic and abstract. Every staging choice in a movement sequence should be justified with reference to the specific emotional moment it represents.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n19\">For an extended response, use the performer or designer perspective named in the question. Make precise choices for that role and explain how each supports the emotional moment.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F13: removes the false combined-response requirement."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 6,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n17\">For the 8-mark &lsquo;Interpret as Designer&rsquo; question, the most common error is describing design ideas in general terms: &ldquo;I would use dark lighting to create a sad mood.&rdquo; This will not reach the top band. You must name specific elements: which lantern (profile, Fresnel, floor birdie), which colour (gel number or descriptive name &mdash; amber, cool blue, steel grey), at what intensity, from which direction, and what specific effect this creates for the audience at a named moment. The mark scheme rewards concreteness. &ldquo;I would use a tight profile spot with a pale amber gel overhead at steep angle to suggest the harsh Australian sun in the summer garden scene, creating a sense of exposure that mirrors the character&rsquo;s emotional vulnerability&rdquo; is the kind of answer that earns Mastering marks.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n17\">When developing a design response, avoid general descriptions such as &ldquo;dark lighting to create a sad mood&rdquo;. Name the design elements, describe how they would be used at a specific moment and explain the effect on the audience.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F15: removes the inaccurate 8-mark design-question claim."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 6,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">For the 20-mark extended staging response, spread your answer across multiple design disciplines: set, lighting, sound AND costume. If you focus only on lighting, you are missing a significant portion of the marks. Each discipline should address the same named moment from a different angle, and you should explain how the design choices work together to create a unified effect on the audience.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n18\">For a 20-mark design response, develop detailed choices in the one design specialism named in the question. Link them to the extract and the whole play, and explain their intended effect on the audience.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F16: corrects the one-specialism format of a design response."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 7,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "The summer scene in which Mark tells his parents that he is transitioning to Mia",
+    "replace": "The winter scene in which Mark tells his parents that he is transitioning to Mia",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F17: Mark's disclosure occurs in winter."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 7,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n19\">For the 20-mark extended staging response, an answer focused on the ending tableau must address both performer choices for named characters AND at least one design discipline. The tableau is not just about position; it is about light (what lighting state holds the final image?), sound (is there underscore as the tableau forms, or silence?) and the specific body language of each character in relation to the others. Think spatially as well as dramatically.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n19\">For a 20-mark response, use the performer or designer perspective named in the question. Keep your choices precise, link them to the extract and the whole play, and explain their intended effect on the audience.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F18: removes the false combined-response requirement."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 8,
+    "field": "content_html",
+    "find": "data-def=\"Stanislavski&rsquo;s term for the overall goal a character is pursuing throughout the entire play; the driving desire that shapes all their actions.\">objective</dfn>",
+    "replace": "data-def=\"Stanislavski&rsquo;s term for a goal a character pursues in a scene or moment.\">objective</dfn>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F19: distinguishes an objective from a super-objective."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 8,
+    "field": "knowledge_checks",
+    "path": "[2].q",
+    "expect": "In Stanislavski's system, the overall goal a character is pursuing throughout the entire play is called their _____.",
+    "value": "In Stanislavski's system, a goal a character pursues in a scene or moment is called their _____.",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F20: objectives are scene-or-moment goals."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 8,
+    "field": "flashcard_questions",
+    "path": "[3].a",
+    "expect": "The overall goal a character is pursuing throughout the play or scene.",
+    "value": "A goal a character pursues in a scene or moment.",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F21: distinguishes an objective from a super-objective."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 8,
+    "field": "glossary_terms",
+    "path": "[3].definition",
+    "expect": "Stanislavski's term for the overall goal a character is pursuing throughout the entire play; the driving desire that shapes all their actions.",
+    "value": "Stanislavski's term for a goal a character pursues in a scene or moment.",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F22: distinguishes an objective from a super-objective."
+  },
+  {
+    "lesson": 8,
+    "field": "exam_tip_html",
+    "find": "<p data-narration-id=\"n19\">For the 20-mark extended staging response, a question about practitioner application is also a staging question: you must address how the technique manifests in performance AND how a designer supports it. The movement sequence and the lighting state that accompanies it are part of the same theatrical statement. Do not describe one without the other.</p>",
+    "replace": "<p data-narration-id=\"n19\">For a 20-mark response, use the performer or designer perspective named in the question. Explain how the chosen technique shapes the performance or design choices for a specific moment and affects the audience.</p>",
+    "severity": "HIGH",
+    "note": "F23: removes the false combined-response requirement."
+  }
+]
